mod sculpt;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use *;
import noise;
import sdf;
import sdfmesh;

//#line 1 "bevel.cone"
// ======== Bevel: edges and corners cut back into strips of faces ========
//
// Blender's Bevel (the edit tool, the modifier and the Mesh Bevel node), edge
// and vertex modes, rebuilt from its manual and the geometry, not its source.
//
// EDGE BEVEL. Each selected edge is replaced by a strip of 'segments' faces
// running along it, flat (a chamfer) or rounded by a profile, and each vertex
// where selected edges end is cut back to fit.
//
// - THE WIDTH is Blender's Offset: in each of the edge's two faces, the strip's
//   side runs parallel to the edge, 'width' from it in the face's plane (times
//   the edge's selection weight, see below). 'BevelWidth' measures the distance
//   between the strip's two sides instead, 'BevelDepth' the distance from the
//   edge to the chord joining them, each turned into an offset per edge by the
//   angle between its faces.
// - AT A VERTEX each face corner that touches a selected edge is moved: where
//   two selected edges meet in a face, to the point where their offset lines
//   cross (a sharp mitre, Blender's Sharp miter, inner and outer alike; lines
//   that are parallel, as along a straight run, give the point straight in);
//   where a selected edge meets an edge that is not selected, along that edge
//   to where the offset line crosses it (Blender's loop slide; when selected
//   edges lie on both sides of it, the mean of the two). An edge that is not
//   selected and has no selected edge beside it keeps the vertex as its end.
// - THE PROFILE across the strip, from the point A in one face to B in the
//   other, is a superellipse in the frame its corner gives: P = (1 - y) A +
//   (1 - x) B + (x + y - 1) C with x^r + y^r = 1, C the profile's corner on the
//   edge's line. Sampled at equal angles (x, y) = (cos t, sin t) / ||(cos t,
//   sin t)||_r, t = 0 to 90 degrees in 'segments' steps, symmetric to the bit.
//   'shape' is Blender's profile shape: 0.5 a circular arc, 1 the original
//   sharp corner, 0 a cove into the opposite corner; r = ln 4 / ln(1 / shape),
//   so 0.25 is a straight chamfer whatever the segments (the profile's middle
//   lies at the square root of 'shape' along the diagonal from the far corner
//   to C).
// - THE CORNER where selected edges meet (the vertex mesh):
//   - one selected edge ending at a vertex of three edges: the strip's end is
//     set into the face beyond it, its profile in that face's plane, as a
//     bevelled cube edge rounds the corner of the end face;
//   - two selected edges passing through or turning at a vertex, with nothing
//     else in the way (an elbow of a cube, or a loop running through a vertex
//     of four edges): the two strips share one profile, a mitred joint;
//   - three selected edges at a vertex of three edges (a box's corner): a patch
//     of quads on the superellipsoid that the three profiles bound, x^r + y^r
//     + z^r = 1 in the frame of the three mitre points about its centre (the
//     point nearest the three edges' profile axes), a grid of quads round a
//     centre point or, for an odd number of segments, a centre triangle
//     (Blender's Grid Fill); exactly an eighth of a sphere at a cube's corner;
//   - anything else, and the box's corner with 'BevelVertexMeshCutoff': one
//     polygon through every new point round the vertex (Blender's Cutoff),
//     or, where the vertex stays as the end of edges in runs apart, one
