// ======== Falloff: the weight of a brush or a selection by distance ========
//
// A 'Falloff' turns d, the distance from a centre as a fraction of a radius,
// into a weight: 1 at the centre, 0 at the radius and beyond. It is one of
// geomath's presets, Blender's brush curves ('FalloffSmooth', 'FalloffSphere',
// 'FalloffRoot', 'FalloffSharp', 'FalloffSmoothSharp', 'FalloffLinear' and
// 'FalloffConstant', and facerig's 'FalloffGauss' and 'FalloffPlateau'; see
// geomath's falloff.cone for each formula), or a custom curve, Blender's
// "Custom" brush curve: control points (d, weight) joined by a monotone cubic
// (Fritsch and Carlson, "Monotone piecewise cubic interpolation", SIAM
// Journal on Numerical Analysis 17(2), 1980, with the slopes at the knots of
// Fritsch and Butland's harmonic mean), which never overshoots its points, so
// a curve drawn through (0, 1) and (1, 0) never rises above 1 or dips below 0.
//
// Before its first point a custom curve is its first weight, after its
// last its last; at d above 1 every falloff is 0, a brush ends at its
// radius. The points may be given in any order; of two with the same d the
// first given is kept.

pub const FalloffCustom = 100u32;

pub struct Falloff {
  pub kind: u32;              // a preset's number, or FalloffCustom
  pub xs: List[f32];          // custom: the knots' d, ascending
  pub ys: List[f32];          // custom: the weights
  pub ms: List[f32];          // custom: the slopes at the knots

  // One of geomath's presets
  pub extern fn preset(kind: u32) Falloff;
//#line 32 "falloff.cone"

  // A custom curve through points (x = d, y = weight)
  pub extern fn custom(points: &Array[Vec2]) Falloff;
//#line 82 "falloff.cone"

  // The weight at distance fraction d
  pub extern fn weight(self: &, d: f32) f32;
//#line 102 "falloff.cone"
}

//#line 1 "fractal.cone"
// ======== Fractal refinement of a 2-D outline ========
//
// A coastline from a coarse outline: each edge is split in two, the split
// point moved off the line by a random amount, and each half split again, down
// to 'levels' levels, so an edge becomes 2^levels pieces with inlets and
// headlands at every scale (Fournier, Fussell and Carpenter's midpoint
// displacement, 1982).
//
// The split point is kept inside a quadrilateral, as Amit Patel's "noisy
// edges" do (Red Blob Games): an edge A B carries two more corners P and Q,
// one either side of it, and its split point M is on the segment from Q to P.
// The half A M then carries the corners A + s (P - A) and A + s (Q - A), and
// the half M B carries B + s (P - B) and B + s (Q - B), where s is the shrink
// (0 < s < 1). Each half's quadrilateral lies inside its parent's, on its own
// side of the line through P and Q, so two pieces of one edge never cross, and
// everything below an edge stays inside that edge's first quadrilateral (which
// is what lets 'fractalRefineWithin' skip, exactly, every edge whose
// quadrilateral misses a window). Patel halves the corners (s = 1/2); here
// s = 2^-h, so a smaller h keeps the quadrilaterals wider as the pieces
// shorten, and the outline rougher.
//
// What the guarantee costs. Every quadrilateral below a base edge keeps that
// edge's Q P direction (a half's corners are its parent's shrunk towards one
// end), so an edge's refinement is a graph over the edge: it never turns back
// on itself, and every wiggle is displaced along the one direction. With
// h < 1 a half's split sits s / 2 of its parent from its own end, past its
// middle, and as the quadrilaterals widen against their pieces the deep
// levels become needles all leaning one way, a comb. At h = 1 (Patel's) the
// outline is even and coast-like.
//
// Every random number is a hash of (seed, edge, level, index within the
// level), never a stream: a piece is the same however much else is made,
// and a coarse refinement's points are exactly the points a finer one keeps
// at the same places. A base edge's first corners are P, Q = its middle plus
// and minus 'width' times its length, square to it.
//
// The dials beyond roughness, each giving up the plain fractal's evenness:
//
// - tail, tailCap: the displacement is noise.heavySigned, so a rare split runs
//   to its quadrilateral's edge (a ratio of 1 is the plain uniform draw).
// - intermittency: a cascade; each piece multiplies the amplitude it hands to
//   its halves by 1 + intermittency u (u in [-1, 1)), so detail clusters.
// - heteroStrength, heteroScale: the amplitude is scaled by
//   max(0, 1 + strength g), g a gradient noise of the split's middle at
//   'heteroScale' cells per unit: calm stretches and wild ones.

// The dials of a fractal refinement
pub struct Fractal {
  pub seed: u32;
  pub levels: i32;          // splits per base edge: 2^levels pieces
  pub shrink: f32;          // the corners' shrink per level, 2^-h, in (0, 1)
  pub amplitude: f32;       // the displacement's scale, as a fraction of the way from the middle of Q P to P
  pub width: f32;           // a base edge's corners, as a fraction of its length
  pub tail: f32;            // heavySigned's ratio: 1 for none
  pub tailCap: i32;         // heavySigned's cap
  pub intermittency: f32;   // the cascade: 0 for none
  pub heteroStrength: f32;  // the heterogeneity field's strength: 0 for none
  pub heteroScale: f32;     // its cells per unit length
}

// A plain refinement: roughness 'h' (held to at least 1/64), a displacement
// 'amplitude', a base width of 0.4, and no tail, cascade or heterogeneity
pub extern fn fractal(seed: u32, levels: i32, h: f32, amplitude: f32) Fractal;
//#line 67 "fractal.cone"

// A point of a refined outline and its place: base edge e's piece i at the
// finest level is e 2^levels + i, and the point is that piece's start
pub struct FractalPoint {
  pub index: u64;
  pub point: Vec2;
}

