//#line 1 "join.cone"
// ======== Join: separately made meshes as one ========

// Join (Blender's Join, and its Join Geometry node): the meshes 'parts' made
// into one PolyMesh, part after part. Part k's vertex v becomes vertex
// V0 + ... + V(k-1) + v (Vj part j's vertex count), and its half-edges and
// faces are numbered on in the same way, every link offset with them, so
// each part keeps its own half-edges, edges, boundary loops and corners
// exactly; nothing is welded or bridged (that is 'weld' and 'bridgeLoops'
// afterwards). Joining a mesh with an empty one gives the mesh back bit for
// bit.
//
// Channels are matched by name and domain, and the result carries the union
// of every part's channels: each type's channels in the order they are first
// met, part by part. A part with a channel gives its own values; the elements
// of a part without it get the channel's fill (see mesh.Channel: 0 by
// default, 1 for a weight), so the joined mesh behaves as each part did
// alone. Where parts give one channel different fills, the first part's is
// kept.
//
// The same name with a different type (f32, Vec2, Vec3) or a different
// domain in two parts is refused, not converted, since it is almost always a
// mistake: the later part's channel is left out (its elements get the fill
// of the channel kept), and a line saying why is added to 'problems', as
// fromPolygons reports a face it leaves out; the join is still made. Each
// part is checked against the channels of the parts before it, so the first
// part's form of a name is the one kept. (The edge "crease" and the vertex
// "crease_vert" are two names, so they never clash.)
pub extern fn join(parts: &List[PolyMesh], problems: &mut List[String]) PolyMesh;
