// ======== Loft: a surface through a sequence of profiles ========
//
// A loft places profiles (stations) one after another and joins them with a
// band of quads, as a NURBS loft or Blender's Bridge Edge Loops over loose
// loops does; 'bridgeLoops' (bridge.cone) joins existing boundary loops of a
// mesh by the same correspondence. Correspondence is the work: the profiles
// may have different point counts and shapes, so each is put on common
// columns, and a closed one is started where it best follows the one before,
// so the band does not twist.
//
// - Orientation: a closed profile is turned counterclockwise (its points
//   reversed after its first, so its start stays), so every station faces
//   the same way and the faces face outward. An open profile is taken as it
//   runs.
// - Start: a closed profile after the first is started at whichever of its
//   points makes it match the station before most closely: both compared
//   about their own centres (the mean of their points), by the sum of
//   squared distances, the least sum winning and the earliest point on a
//   tie. Profiles of one count compare point with point (a cyclic shift, as
//   Blender's bridge pairs vertices); otherwise each is sampled evenly by
//   length, 2 x the larger count of samples. The first profile keeps its own
//   start.
// - Columns ('count'): 0 with every profile of one count joins point to
//   point, the profiles' own correspondence (a hull's keel to keel, chine to
//   chine); 0 with counts that differ puts every ring on the union of all the
//   profiles' points' fractions of length, so each profile keeps every one of
//   its points and only gains points on its sides; a positive count samples
//   every profile at that many points evenly by length (corners may be cut).
//   A fraction within LoftSnap of a profile's own point is that point, bit
//   for bit.
//
// A profile of one point (an apex: a bow, a tip) is allowed where the counts
// differ: every column meets at it.

// Fractions of length this close are one: a sample this near a profile's own
// point is that point
const LoftSnap = 0.00001f;

//#line 42 "loft.cone"

//#line 396 "loft.cone"
// A surface through the profiles, profile k placed in frame k (its x on the
// frame's normal, its y on its binormal, as a sweep places its profile), in
// order (Blender's Bridge Edge Loops over loose loops, a NURBS loft). The
// profiles are put on common columns and started so the band does not twist
// (see the top of this file; 'count' chooses the columns); a closed first
// profile makes every ring close round. Neighbouring stations are joined by
// quads, with 'between' more rings between each pair, on the straight lines
// joining their columns' points or, with 'smooth', on the Catmull-Rom spline
// through each column's points (its knots the stations' "along"). With
// 'caps', closed rings' two ends are closed by an n-gon each. Faces face
// outward when the stations advance along their frames' tangents (normal x
// binormal), as a sweep's do. If the lists differ in length, the shorter
// decides; fewer than 2 stations give the one ring with no faces.
//
// Numbering: ring by ring from the first station, n vertices a ring (n the
// columns), ring r's column j vertex r n + j; quads as 'extrude''s, then the
// caps. A station's "along" is its distance along the polyline of the
// frames' origins over that polyline's length (equal steps when it has
// none), a ring between stations its blend; "around" is the column's
// fraction of length, point to point each ring's own (blended between
// stations), and "uv" is (around, along) at each corner, the closing
// corners of a closed ring at u = 1; caps map from the end rings' boxes, as
// a sweep's.
