//#line 1 "subdivide.cone"
// ======== Subdivision: Catmull-Clark ========
//
// Catmull and Clark, "Recursively generated B-spline surfaces on arbitrary
// topological meshes", CAD 10(6), 1978, with semi-sharp creases after DeRose,
// Kass and Truong, "Subdivision surfaces in character animation", SIGGRAPH
// 1998, and the boundary, crease and face-varying rules as Pixar's OpenSubdiv
// documents them (its "Subdivision Surfaces" overview; the algorithm only, no
// code). One level turns every face of n corners into n quads, so after the
// first level the mesh is all quads, whatever mix of triangles, quads and
// n-gons it began as.
//
// One level makes three kinds of new vertex:
// - a face point for each face: the mean of its corners;
// - an edge point for each edge: on a smooth edge, the mean of its two ends
//   and the face points of its two faces, (a + b + F0 + F1) / 4; on a sharp
//   edge (a boundary edge, or a crease of sharpness 1 or more), its midpoint
//   (a + b) / 2; on a crease of sharpness s between 0 and 1, the smooth point
//   and the midpoint blended, s of the midpoint;
// - a vertex point for each vertex, by one of four rules chosen by how many
//   sharp edges meet there (a crease of any sharpness above 0 counts):
//   - smooth (none) and dart (one): (Q + 2R + (n - 3) S) / n, where n is its
//     valence, S where it is, Q the mean of its faces' face points and R the
//     mean of its edges' midpoints; that is
//     ((n - 2) S + (sum of its neighbours) / n + (sum of its face points) / n) / n;
//   - crease (two): (A + 6 S + B) / 8, A and B the far ends of the two sharp
//     edges: a cubic B-spline along the crease;
//   - corner (three or more, or a sharp vertex): S, it stays.
//   When a semi-sharp crease wears out at this level (its sharpness is 1 or
//   less, so its child is smooth), the rule its sharpness gives now and the
//   one its children's gives are blended, by the mean sharpness of the edges
//   (and vertex) that wear out, at most 1 (OpenSubdiv's fractional weight).
//
// Sharpness: an edge's is the edge channel of f32 named "crease", a vertex's
// the vertex channel named "crease_vert", as Blender names them (0 where
// there is none). Its values are
// OpenSubdiv's (and USD's creaseSharpnesses): 0 is smooth, s is sharp for s
// levels, and SharpnessInfinite (10) or more is sharp at every level. A
// child edge of a parent edge gets the parent's sharpness less 1 (at least
// 0; an infinite one stays), OpenSubdiv's uniform creasing; the edges made
// inside a face get 0. A vertex point's vertex sharpness goes down the same
// way.
//
// Boundaries: a boundary edge is infinitely sharp, so it subdivides as a cubic
// B-spline curve, and a boundary vertex with more than one face takes the
// crease rule along it. 'BoundaryEdgeAndCorner', the default (USD's
// interpolateBoundary default, edgeAndCorner, and RenderMan's; Blender's
// "Keep Corners"), also keeps a corner, a boundary vertex with one face, where
// it is. 'BoundaryEdgeOnly' (OpenSubdiv's VTX_BOUNDARY_EDGE_ONLY; Blender's
// Boundary Smooth "All", its modifier's default) smooths the corner along its
// boundary curve instead.
//
// Face-varying data, every corner channel ("uv" among them), is subdivided by
// the same rules on its own topology: where the values of the corners at an
// edge's ends differ across it (a uv seam) the edge is a face-varying
// boundary, so each uv island is subdivided as its own mesh with its own
// boundary, and seams stay seams. The corners about a vertex are cut into
// fans by those edges; a fan is subdivided as a boundary vertex is. How its
// boundary is held is one of USD's faceVaryingLinearInterpolation choices
// (OpenSubdiv's FVAR_LINEAR_*):
// - 'FvarLinearNone': smooth everywhere, boundaries as B-spline curves;
// - 'FvarLinearCornersOnly': the same, but a fan of one face keeps its value
//   (a uv island's corner stays put);
// - 'FvarLinearCornersPlus1', the default (USD's): also keeps the value where
//   three or more fans meet at a vertex;
// - 'FvarLinearBoundaries': every value on a face-varying boundary is kept,
//   and its edges' values are midpoints (Blender's UV Smooth default, "Keep
//   Boundaries");
// - 'FvarLinearAll': linear everywhere (OpenSubdiv's Sdc default): corners
//   keep their values, edge values are midpoints, face values means.
//
// Other channels: a vertex channel is subdivided with the vertex rules, as the
// positions are (USD's "vertex" interpolation); a face channel's value passes
// to each of the face's child faces; an edge channel's (other than "crease")
// to each of the edge's two child edges, and the edges made inside a face get
// 0. A mesh needs no channels at all.
//
// The new mesh's vertices are numbered: first the vertex points, vertex v of
// the old mesh as vertex v; then the edge points, edge e as vertex V + e;
// then the face points, face f as V + E + f, where V and E are the old mesh's
// vertex and edge counts. So a cage's vertices keep their numbers at every
// level. Face f of n corners becomes n faces, numbered on from the child
// faces of the faces before it, one for each of its corners in order from its
// first; the child at the corner of vertex v, with the half-edge h ending at
// v, runs (v's vertex point, the edge point of next(h)'s edge, f's face point,
// the edge point of h's edge), counterclockwise as its parent is.

pub const BoundaryEdgeOnly = 0u32;
pub const BoundaryEdgeAndCorner = 1u32;

pub const FvarLinearNone = 0u32;
