// ======== A surface to search: nearest point, nearest vertex, rays ========
//
// The search inside voxel remesh made public, and widened: 'SurfaceIndex.of'
// cuts a PolyMesh's faces into triangles (a triangle is kept as it is, a
// convex face fanned, any other face ear-clipped, as 'triangulate' does) and
// builds two trees of boxes over them (bvh.cone): one over the triangles, one
// over the vertices the triangles use. The mesh need not be closed or
// manifold, and may have faces of no area: a surface to snap to, not a solid.
// Its questions, each answered by a walk down the tree that skips every box
// further than the best answer so far:
//
// - 'nearest': the point of the surface nearest a point, with its triangle,
//   face, barycentric weights, distance and the triangle's unit normal.
//   Ericson's nearest point on a triangle (Real-Time Collision Detection,
//   5.1.5, in remesh.cone), in f64; the least distance wins, and equal
//   distances go to the lowest triangle number, so the answer does not depend
//   on the tree.
// - 'nearestVertex': the nearest vertex that a triangle uses, as a hit on one
//   of its triangles with weight 1 on that vertex.
// - 'ray': the first triangle a ray meets, by the Moller-Trumbore test in
//   f64 with the triangle's plane normal deciding which side faces the ray;
//   'CullFront' ignores triangles facing the ray's origin (as Blender's
//   Shrinkwrap "Cull Faces: Front"), 'CullBack' those facing away. A ray that
//   runs along a shared edge hits one of its triangles, not neither (the
//   barycentric test lets a rounding error of 1e-9 through).
// - 'targetNormal': the point whose interpolated vertex normal line passes
//   through the query point (Blender's Shrinkwrap "Target Normal Project"),
//   found for each candidate triangle by Gauss-Newton on the barycentric
//   coordinates from the triangle's centre; the candidates are the triangles
//   within the limit, or with no limit within twice the nearest distance; the
//   nearest solution wins, and when no triangle has one the nearest point is
//   answered.
// - 'smoothNormal': a hit's normal interpolated from the vertex normals (the
//   angle-weighted normals PolyMesh.vertexNormals gives), unit.
//
// A hit with 'tri' NoIndex found nothing. A limit of 0 or less means no limit.
// DETERMINISTIC: the walks are in a fixed order and every comparison is on
// numbers, so the same mesh and query give the same bits.

pub const CullNone = 0u32;
pub const CullFront = 1u32;
pub const CullBack = 2u32;

