// ======== Data transfer: channels carried from one surface to another ========
//
// Blender's Data Transfer modifier. The channels of a source mesh ('src') are
// copied to a destination mesh ('dst', which keeps its own shape and every
// channel it has): each destination element looks up where it lands on the
// source, and takes the source's values there. A source channel keeps its name,
// domain and fill; a destination channel of the same name and domain is
// written over (or mixed with by 'factor'), and a name that is another type in
// the destination is reported and left. All four domains: a vertex channel
// goes to the destination's vertices, an edge channel to its edges, a face
// channel to its faces, a corner channel to its corners, each by the mapping
// chosen for that domain:
//
// - MapNearest: the source element of the same kind nearest. A vertex takes
//   the nearest source vertex; an edge the nearest edge of the face the
//   nearest surface point is on; a face that face; a corner the corner of
//   that face whose vertex is nearest (Blender's Only Neighbor, Nearest Edge
//   Best Matching, Nearest Face, Nearest Corner).
// - MapInterpolated: a vertex or corner value blended across the triangle the
//   nearest surface point is on, by the point's barycentric weights (Blender's
//   Nearest Face Interpolated). A face value cannot be blended, and an edge's
//   two ends are not a place the nearest point reaches, so those two take the
//   nearest face and nearest edge as MapNearest does.
// - MapProjected: as MapInterpolated, but the point is where a ray from the
//   destination element along its normal (the vertex normal; a face's normal;
//   an edge's the mean of its ends'; a corner's its vertex's), forwards or
//   backwards, whichever meets the source first, meets it (Blender's
//   Projected Face Interpolated); where the ray misses, the nearest surface
//   point serves.
// - MapBestNormal: among the source triangles near the point (within twice the
//   nearest distance), the one whose normal agrees best with the destination
//   element's, then as MapInterpolated (Blender's Best Matching Face Normal).
// Where the element is: a vertex or corner at its vertex, an edge at its
// middle, a face at its centroid. 'maxDistance' (0 or less: none) leaves an
// element farther from the source than that as it was (or at the channel's
// fill, where the destination had no such channel).
//
// 'factor' mixes: the new value is the destination's old one plus factor times
// the difference, the old one being the channel's fill where there is none; 1,
// the default, replaces.
//
// THE CORE, shared with voxel remesh: a 'Sample' is where a destination
// element lands, up to three source elements and the weights that blend them
// (all of a direct pick's weight on one); 'carryChannels' writes every source
// channel along a list of samples per domain. Voxel remesh, which carries the
// nearest point's values to its new elements, builds its samples from the
// nearest triangle and calls the same function.

pub const MapNearest = 0u32;
pub const MapInterpolated = 1u32;
pub const MapProjected = 2u32;
pub const MapBestNormal = 3u32;

// The mappings per domain, the reach and the mix
pub struct TransferMap {
  pub vertex: u32;
  pub edge: u32;
  pub face: u32;
  pub corner: u32;
  pub maxDistance: f32;
  pub factor: f32;

  // One mapping for every domain, no reach limit, replacing
  pub extern fn all(method: u32) TransferMap;
}

