//#line 1 "unsubdivide.cone"
// ======== Un-subdivide: a grid's alternate edge loops removed ========
//
// Un-subdivide (Blender's Decimate modifier in Un-Subdivide mode) undoes a
// regular subdivision's connectivity: one iteration finds the three kinds of
// vertex one level of Catmull-Clark (or any split of each face into quads
// about a middle point) makes, and keeps only the first:
// - a vertex point, the old vertex (V);
// - an edge point, on an old edge, between two vertex points (E);
// - a face point, in the middle of an old face, its quads round it (F).
// Every quad of one level runs V, E, F, E. Each face point's fan of quads
// becomes one face, its corners the vertex points round it; an edge point
// between two such faces (or on the boundary beside one) goes, its two
// sides becoming one. The vertices kept keep their positions: un-subdivide
// gives the cage's connectivity, not its positions (it does not invert the
// smoothing), as Blender's keeps the remaining vertices where they are.
//
// Reading the grid: the vertices are labelled by propagation through the
// quads, from a seed: a vertex point's or a face point's quad gives the
// vertex across it the other of the two and its two neighbours E. Each
// region of quads is read four ways (its lowest-numbered unlabelled vertex a
// vertex point, a face point, or an edge point beside its lowest-numbered
// neighbour read either way), and the reading under which the most quads
// collapse is kept, the earlier on a tie. So the first reading, the lowest
// vertex a vertex point, wins wherever both readings collapse everything
// (on a closed mesh vertex points and face points can swap roles: the cube
// subdivided once is also the octahedron subdivided once); 'subdivide'
// numbers vertex points first, so its output always returns to its cage's
// connectivity, numbering and face order included. 'unsubdivideWith' names
// vertices to keep, which settles a reading where the numbering would not.
//
// What is not on a grid stays: a face point collapses only when it is
// inside the mesh, every face round it is a quad labelled V, E, F, E with
// no vertex labelled two ways, and its loop passes no vertex twice; an edge
// point goes only when every face on it collapsed and it is on four edges
// (two on the boundary, three edges). Every other face is kept as it was,
// and an edge point kept beside a collapsed face stays a corner of it. Each
// iteration that leaves faces reports how many, and one that finds nothing
// to undo reports it and stops.
//
// Channels, by 'assemble': a kept vertex's values are its own; a collapsed
// face's are its fan's lowest-numbered quad's; its corners' those at the same
// vertex in the quad they came from (an edge point kept as a corner, the
// first quad's that has it); an edge made of two halves takes the
// lower-numbered half's values (subdivision gave both its parent's, a
// "crease" one less: un-subdivide does not add it back); a kept face keeps
// everything.
//
// Numbering: the kept vertices keep their order; faces are made in the
// order of their first (lowest-numbered) face, a collapsed face from the
// vertex point of its lowest-numbered quad, round its fan the way its quads
// face.

// One label for each vertex of the grid
const UnsubNone = 0u32;
const UnsubV = 1u32;
const UnsubE = 2u32;
const UnsubF = 3u32;

// The mesh un-subdivided 'iterations' times, each halving a grid made by one
// level of subdivision (see above); what is not on a grid is kept and
// reported. 0 iterations gives a copy.
pub extern fn unsubdivide(m: &PolyMesh, iterations: u32, problems: &mut List[String]) PolyMesh;
//#line 66 "unsubdivide.cone"

